          %p
            With the rise of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/analytic+philosophy"} analytic philosophy
            in Britain, and its adoption and development of the new logic forged by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Frege"} Frege
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peano"} Peano
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idealism"} idealism
            of
            %span.newWikiWord
              T. H. Green
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/T.+H.+Green"} ?
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %span.newWikiWord
                F. H. Bradley
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/F.+H.+Bradley"} ?
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hegel"} Hegel
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bertrand+Russell"} Bertrand Russell
            was adamant that the new logic would thoroughly transform philosophy, as he made clear at the end of his chapter
            = succeed ":" do
              %em Logic As The Essence Of Philosophy
          %blockquote
            %p The old logic put thought in fetters, while the new logic gives it wings. It has, in my opinion, introduced the same kind of advance into philosophy as Galileo introduced into physics, making it possible at last to see what kinds of problems may be capable of solution, and what kinds are beyond human powers. And where a solution appears possible, the new logic provides a method which enables us to obtain results that do not merely embody personal idiosyncrasies, but must command the assent of all who are competent to form an opinion. (Logic As The Essence Of Philosophy, 1914)
          %p
            In the same chapter, Hegel was taken to task for his attempt to widen logic to a form of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metaphysics"} metaphysics
          %p
            This set the tone for analytic philosophy’s opinion of Hegel for several decades. In 1951, the logical empiricist
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hans+Reichenbach"} Hans Reichenbach
            wrote
          %blockquote
            %p Hegel has been called the successor of Kant; that is a serious misunderstanding of Kant and an unjustified elevation of Hegel. Kant’s system, though proved untenable by later developments, was the attempt of a great mind to establish rationalism on a scientific basis. Hegel’s system is the poor construction of a fanatic who has seen one empirical truth and attempts to make it a logical law within the most unscientific of all logics. Whereas Kant’s system marks the peak of the historical line of rationalism, Hegel’s system belongs in the period of decay of speculative philosophy which characterizes the nineteenth century. (Reichenbach 1951, p. 72)
          %p
            However, despite the initial lengthy rejection of Hegelian ideas by analytic philosophy, there have recently been increasing signs of a willingness to re-engage with Hegel’s writings, in particular on the part of
            %span.newWikiWord
              John MacDowell
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/John+MacDowell"} ?
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+Brandom"} Robert Brandom
            (Redding, 2007). Interestingly, one feature of analytic philosophy which needs reconsidering according to Redding is the primacy of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/first-order+logic"} first-order
            predicate logic, not, however, that he is promoting an alternative formalization based on Hegel’s ideas.
          %p
            It took a mathematician
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/William+Lawvere"} William Lawvere
            to find something formalizable of value in Hegel’s
            = succeed "," do
              %em Logic
            = succeed "\u2019." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} Unity of Opposites
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+logic"} categorical logic
            and Hegel’s logic, we will pursue this connection right up to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type+theory"} modal
            forms of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %p
            Now, Hegel’s understanding of the term ‘Logik’ was much broader than is normally the case. Indeed, the ‘Objective logic’ of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Science+of+Logic"} Science of Logic
            is more akin to a kind of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metaphysics"} metaphysics
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vienna+Circle"} Vienna Circle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positivism"} positivist
            attitude towards
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/metaphysics"} metaphysics
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#Carnap32"} Carnap 32
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+logic"} modal logic
            as a tool for philosophy, especially due to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saul+Kripke"} Saul Kripke
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %em Naming and Necessity
          %p
            Today, standard topics for analytic metaphysics today include causality, necessity, space and time, identity, and mental states. Little attention is paid to the question of explaining the kind of physical principles which operate in our universe. It is noteworthy, however, that the need for some such questioning seems to be as strongly felt among modern physicists as it must have been to Hegel and his predecessors, as witnessed by … debates such as critically reviewed in (
            %a{:href => "#Albert12"}> Albert 12
            ). (…) Attempts to re-install this kind of metaphysics are these days undertaken by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/particle+physics"} particle physicists
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cosmology"} cosmologists
            themselves, whose success at formally describing remote aspects of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observable+universe"} observable universe
            has emboldened many to feel as superior to modern philosophy as modern philosophy typically feels towards Hegel’ idealism.
          %p (…)
          %h2#modern_foundations__types_judgements_and_deduction Modern foundations – Types, Judgements and Deduction
          %p (…) something like:
          %p
            analytic philosophy was certainly right to ask for a formal basis of philosophical reasoning, in order to make genuine intellectual progress possible. Back then
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/predicate+logic"} predicate logic
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set+theory"} set theory
            was the state of the art concerning the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/foundations+of+mathematics"} foundations of mathematics
            and so this is what analytic philosophy adopted and developed. But since then there has been much progress in the foundations of maths. …
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intuitionistic+type+theory"} intuitionistic type theory
            …
            = succeed "\u2026" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %p Accordingly, the original starting point of analytic philosophy needs to be re-examined. While predicate logic has no way of making formal sense of Hegel’s “Logic”, for type theory the situation is rather different…
          %h2#categorical_semantics_and_categorical_logic Categorical semantics and Categorical logic
          %p … type theory proper is a formal system for symbol manipulation … useful for computer encoding, and much more readable than full formalization in, say, ZFC, but still not really what a discussion such as our here would directly work with…
          %p
            … instead, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/syntax"} syntax
            of type theory has
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/semantics"} semantics
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            –
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categorical+semantics"} categorical semantics
            …
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+category+theory+and+type+theory"} relation between category theory and type theory
            – and this semantic level is what we will be concerned with here
          %p
            … originates with Lawvere’s thesis, see also “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Adjointness+in+Foundations"}> Adjointness in Foundations
            ”
          %h2#modalities_moments_and_opposites Modalities, moments and opposites
          %p
            (… basics of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modalities"} modalities
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operators"} modal operators
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monads"} monads
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+modality"} adjoint modality
            = succeed "\u2026)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unity+of+opposites"} unity of opposites
          %h2#formal_determinations_of_being Formal determinations of Being
          %p … using this and building on Lawvere’s lead, we are led to objectively investigate the following:
          %p
            starting with bare homotopy type theory and adding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/adjoint+modalities"} adjoint modalities
            to it, what is the nature of the new propositions that can be proven with these modalities, hence what is the new “quality” of the types in the presence of these new axioms…
